Here's a JavaScript code snippet that removes the `(age X)` part from a string like `"july 7 1994 (age 7) London, UK (1994-07-07)"`, where the number inside `age` can vary:

```javascript
function removeAge(text) {
  // Regular expression to match "(age <any number>)"
  return text.replace(/\(age\s*\d+\)\s*/gi, '');
}

// Example usage
const input = "july 7 1994 (age 7) London, UK (1994-07-07)";
const output = removeAge(input);

console.log(output); // Output: "july 7 1994 London, UK (1994-07-07)"
```

### Explanation:
- `/\(age\s*\d+\)\s*/gi` breaks down as:
  - `\(age`: matches literal `(age`
  - `\s*`: matches optional whitespace
  - `\d+`: matches one or more digits
  - `\)\s*`: matches the closing `)` and optional following whitespace
  - `g`: global flag — removes all occurrences
  - `i`: case-insensitive (in case "Age" is capitalized)

### More Examples:
```javascript
console.log(removeAge("born on (age 15) New York")); 
// Output: "born on New York"

console.log(removeAge("event happened (age 23) in Paris (age 5)")); 
// Output: "event happened in Paris"
```

This will remove all `(age X)` substrings regardless of the number.

